# WITHOUT THIS THE IMAGE DOES NOT BUILD, and the failure names the wrong cause.
#
# The Dockerfile restores from the .csproj files first and then `COPY src/ src/`, which is the standard
# layer-caching shape. But that second COPY also carries the HOST's obj/ directories, and they land on
# top of the ones the container's own restore just wrote. The host's project.assets.json points at
# /home/<user>/.nuget/packages, a path that does not exist in the image, so `dotnet publish --no-restore`
# reports:
#
#   error NETSDK1064: Package Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi, version 10.0.10 was not found. It might
#   have been deleted since NuGet restore.
#
# It was never deleted. A restore that worked was overwritten by one that describes another machine --
# which is why the message sends you looking at NuGet feeds and package versions instead of at COPY.
#
# The whole suite passing says nothing about this: `dotnet test` never reads these files through Docker.
obj/
bin/
**/obj/
**/bin/

# Build output and local state that must not travel into the image.
.vs/
.vscode/
*.user

# Secrets. .env feeds docker compose on the HOST and is resolved into environment variables before the
# build starts; a copy inside the image would be a credential baked into a layer for no reason.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The API image has no use for the tests, and copying them would rebuild the layer on every test edit.
tests/

# Agent and planning artifacts. .superpowers is already gitignored; naming it here keeps a local
# checkout that has one from silently enlarging the build context.
.git/
.github/
.superpowers/
.claude/
.agents/
.atl/
